.TH ECVT_R 3
.SH NAME
ecvt_r, fcvt_r, qecvt_r, qfcvt_r \- reentrant conversion of a floating value to a digit string
.SH LIBRARY
Standard C library
.RI ( libc ", " \-lc )
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.BI "int ecvt_r(double " value ", int " ndigit ", int *" decpt ,
.BI "int *" sign ", char *" buf ", size_t " len ");"
.br
.BI "int fcvt_r(double " value ", int " ndigit ", int *" decpt ,
.BI "int *" sign ", char *" buf ", size_t " len ");"
.br
.BI "int qecvt_r(long double " value ", int " ndigit ", int *" decpt ,
.BI "int *" sign ", char *" buf ", size_t " len ");"
.br
.BI "int qfcvt_r(long double " value ", int " ndigit ", int *" decpt ,
.BI "int *" sign ", char *" buf ", size_t " len ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
These are the reentrant equivalents of the obsolete
.BR ecvt (3)
and
.BR fcvt (3)
conversions: they write the bare decimal digits of
.I value
into the caller-supplied
.IR buf
(of size
.IR len )
rather than into static storage.
.PP
For
.B ecvt_r
(and the long-double
.BR qecvt_r ),
.I ndigit
is the total number of significant digits.  For
.B fcvt_r
(and
.BR qfcvt_r )
it is the number of digits after the decimal point.
.PP
The position of the decimal point relative to the start of the returned digit
string is stored in
.IR *decpt :
a value of
.I n
means the point falls after the first
.I n
digits (it may be zero or negative).
.I *sign
is set nonzero if
.I value
is negative, zero otherwise.
.SH RETURN VALUE
These functions return 0 on success, or \-1 if
.I buf
is NULL or
.I len
is zero.
.SH NOTES
.BR ecvt (3)
and
.BR fcvt (3)
are marked obsolete in POSIX; new code should use
.BR snprintf (3)
with
.B %e
or
.BR %f .
For a value whose rounding carries into an extra leading digit, the exact digit
count may differ from other implementations by one; the represented value is the
same.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR snprintf (3),
.BR strtod (3)
.SH STANDARDS
GNU extension (the reentrant forms of the SVID
.BR ecvt (3)/ fcvt (3)).
